WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.293 --> 00:00:05.172
The next morning Jean Valjean returned to the Gillenormand house.

2
00:00:05.173 --> 00:00:11.296
Marius and Cosette had invited him to live with them, but he had come for another reason.

3
00:00:11.297 --> 00:00:18.121
"We missed you at the wedding banquet," said Marius, coming into the parlor. "Will you have breakfast with us today?"

4
00:00:18.122 --> 00:00:23.903
"Monsieur, I have something to tell you," blurted out Valjean. "I’m a former convict."

5
00:00:23.904 --> 00:00:27.351
"What does this mean?" Marius looked puzzled.

6
00:00:27.352 --> 00:00:35.772
"It means I spent 19 years on a prison ship. I stole a loaf of bread and tried to escape from prison.

7
00:00:35.773 --> 00:00:44.757
Then I was sentenced to life in prison for another robbery. If anyone knew I was alive, there would be a warrant for my arrest."

8
00:00:44.758 --> 00:00:50.835
"But you’re Cosette’s father." Marius could not believe what he was hearing.

9
00:00:50.836 --> 00:00:56.851
"I’ve been like a father to Cosette, but I’m not her real father.

10
00:00:56.852 --> 00:01:02.899
My name is not Fauchelevent; I am Jean Valjean."

11
00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:08.065
Then Valjean told the whole history of his relationship with Cosette.

12
00:01:08.066 --> 00:01:14.581
A wave of shock and horror washed over Marius. "Why are you telling me all this?"

13
00:01:14.582 --> 00:01:25.067
"Out of honor. I could live under a false identity as long as I was protecting Cosette. But I can’t continue to do it just for myself."

14
00:01:25.068 --> 00:01:29.233
Marius shook his head. "I still don’t understand."

15
00:01:29.234 --> 00:01:33.375
"Imagine that one day the three of us are chatting and laughing in the park.

16
00:01:33.376 --> 00:01:40.709
Suddenly you hear a voice shout, ‘Jean Valjean!’ And then the police spring out of nowhere and arrest me."

17
00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:44.796
"But no one knows you’re alive," protested Marius.

18
00:01:44.797 --> 00:01:49.876
"I still must do this for myself, to clear my conscience."

19
00:01:49.877 --> 00:01:58.939
At that moment Cosette entered the room. "What are you two talking about—politics? Men are always talking about politics."

20
00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:04.527
"No, my love, not politics," said Marius, as Valjean looked sadly at Cosette.

21
00:02:04.528 --> 00:02:11.273
"Come, both of you, it’s time for breakfast!" said Cosette gaily. "I’ll wait for you in the dining room."

22
00:02:11.274 --> 00:02:17.440
After she had gone, Marius murmured, "Poor Cosette. When she finds out—"

23
00:02:17.441 --> 00:02:23.621
Valjean trembled at these words. "I beg you not to tell her. She would be horrified."

24
00:02:23.622 --> 00:02:28.821
Valjean sank into a chair and tears rolled down his cheeks.

25
00:02:28.822 --> 00:02:34.416
"I’ll go away and never see her again, but please don’t tell her."

26
00:02:34.417 --> 00:02:42.337
"I give you my word that I’ll keep your secret," said Marius. "But you must do what you think is right regarding seeing her."

27
00:02:42.338 --> 00:02:47.749
Valjean sighed. "Oh, I don’t think I could go without seeing her.

28
00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:50.487
Please allow me to come occasionally."

29
00:02:50.488 --> 00:02:55.558
So a routine was established, and Valjean visited Cosette every day.

30
00:02:55.559 --> 00:03:01.066
She was puzzled by his insistence on meeting in a basement room instead of the parlor.

31
00:03:01.067 --> 00:03:08.066
But Valjean reminded her that he’d always been odd, and this was just his latest eccentricity.

32
00:03:08.067 --> 00:03:12.489
In truth, he didn’t think he was worthy of meeting her in the parlor.

33
00:03:12.490 --> 00:03:17.471
Marius always made sure to be out of the house whenever Valjean arrived.

34
00:03:17.472 --> 00:03:23.550
One day Marius and Cosette went to visit the garden at the Rue Plumet house.

35
00:03:23.551 --> 00:03:31.796
They spent a happy morning remembering their secret meetings. Later that day Cosette described their visit to Valjean.

36
00:03:31.797 --> 00:03:34.367
"How did you get there?" he asked.

37
00:03:34.368 --> 00:03:37.621
"We walked to the house and took a cab home."

38
00:03:37.622 --> 00:03:43.304
"Why don’t you have a carriage of your own? You should enjoy being rich."

39
00:03:43.305 --> 00:03:48.455
"I don’t know," replied Cosette. "We’re happy with what we have."

40
00:03:48.456 --> 00:03:54.113
In reality Marius didn’t want to spend any of the money that Valjean had given them.

41
00:03:54.114 --> 00:04:01.930
He was sure that Valjean must have obtained it illegally. Marius was determined to return the money to its rightful owner.

42
00:04:01.931 --> 00:04:08.595
Little by little, Marius cut Cosette off from Valjean. Cosette made no objections.

43
00:04:08.596 --> 00:04:14.930
Besides, she was so in love with her husband that she focused all her attention on him.

44
00:04:14.931 --> 00:04:22.589
As for Valjean, he began to grow ill and weak. By the summer he was rarely visiting Cosette.

45
00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:29.422
One day Marius received a letter from a man announcing that he knew a secret about Jean Valjean.

46
00:04:29.423 --> 00:04:37.740
Marius recognized the handwriting and the paper that smelled of tobacco. The letter was from Thenardier.

47
00:04:37.741 --> 00:04:40.218
Marius agreed to see him.

48
00:04:40.219 --> 00:04:48.486
"I’d like to live in America," announced Thenardier, "but I need a little money."

49
00:04:48.487 --> 00:04:50.732
"How does that concern me?"

50
00:04:50.733 --> 00:04:58.374
"I have a secret to tell you. Monsieur, your father-in-law is an assassin and a robber.

51
00:04:58.375 --> 00:05:02.216
He’s really a former convict named Jean Valjean."

52
00:05:02.217 --> 00:05:06.740
"So? I already know that, and I also know who you are.

53
00:05:06.741 --> 00:05:13.629
You’re a scoundrel and criminal, who goes by Jondrette or whatever name pleases you at the moment.

54
00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:19.560
As for your secret, I know more about it than you do because I’ve investigated Valjean myself.

55
00:05:19.561 --> 00:05:22.796
He robbed a rich factory owner named Madeleine.

56
00:05:22.797 --> 00:05:29.542
As for the murder, I was in the barricade when he killed a spy, a policeman named Javert."

57
00:05:29.543 --> 00:05:41.526
Thenardier shook his head. "Valjean is Madeleine; they are the same person. And he didn’t kill Javert; the policeman committed suicide."

58
00:05:41.527 --> 00:05:42.793
"What?"

59
00:05:42.794 --> 00:05:48.423
"I knew you wouldn’t believe me, so I brought these newspaper articles."

60
00:05:48.424 --> 00:05:52.836
Thenardier drew some papers from his pocket and spread them on the table.

61
00:05:52.837 --> 00:05:58.177
Marius studied the newspapers and a smile spread across his face.

62
00:05:58.178 --> 00:06:08.257
"So this unhappy man is really a wonderful man! And that fortune is his own! He didn’t kill Javert; he spared the spy’s life!"

63
00:06:08.258 --> 00:06:18.156
"No, he’s still a murderer and a robber," insisted Thenardier. "During the uprising last June, I found myself in the sewers of Paris.

64
00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:26.817
Please, do not ask why. Imagine my surprise when I came upon Valjean with a corpse slung over his shoulder.

65
00:06:26.818 --> 00:06:35.158
I didn’t recognize him at first, but it was obvious he’d killed this rich young man and was looking for a way to dispose of the body.

66
00:06:35.159 --> 00:06:44.476
I managed to cut off a piece of the dead man’s coat." Thenardier pulled a piece of cloth from his pocket. "And here it is."

67
00:06:44.477 --> 00:06:48.230
Marius turned pale.

68
00:06:48.231 --> 00:06:53.722
He walked to a closet and removed a black coat with part of the hem missing.

69
00:06:53.723 --> 00:07:03.073
"I was that corpse, and here is the coat!" Marius took money from his pocket and flung it at Thenardier. "Get out of here!

70
00:07:03.074 --> 00:07:08.706
The only thing that keeps me from turning you over to the police is that I know what you did at Waterloo!"

71
00:07:08.707 --> 00:07:13.206
As soon as Thenardier was gone, Marius called for Cosette.

72
00:07:13.207 --> 00:07:19.301
"We must go to your father at once. He was the man who saved my life at the barricade!"

73
00:07:19.302 --> 00:07:25.619
When they arrived at the apartment on the Rue de l’Homme Armé, Valjean was sitting in a chair.

74
00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:30.261
"Cosette!" he said weakly. "Is it really you?"

75
00:07:30.262 --> 00:07:34.857
"Father . . . ," began Marius before he broke into tears.

76
00:07:34.858 --> 00:07:39.611
Valjean gazed at him. "Then you’ve forgiven me?"

77
00:07:39.612 --> 00:07:46.182
Marius nodded while Cosette reached for Valjean’s hand. "How cold you are, Father."

78
00:07:46.183 --> 00:07:54.579
"I’m dying. I’ve written you a note explaining everything and leaving you my silver candlesticks."

79
00:07:54.580 --> 00:07:57.674
"You can’t die!" cried Cosette.

80
00:07:57.675 --> 00:08:01.095
"You’re too strong!" said Marius.

81
00:08:01.096 --> 00:08:12.912
But Valjean was growing weaker by the minute. "Come closer so that I can die happy. Let me put my hands on your beloved heads."

82
00:08:12.913 --> 00:08:20.476
He looked up toward heaven, and Cosette and Marius fell on their knees, overwhelmed with grief.

83
00:08:20.477 --> 00:08:30.109
Without doubt, an angel stood with outstretched wings, awaiting the soul of this virtuous man.

